 "lawn": {
  "years": [
   2022,
   2024
  ],
  "surveys": [
   "TX_Coastal_B1_2018",
   "TX_Coastal_B3_2018",
   "TX_Coastal_B2_Z15_2018",
   "TX_Hurricane_B5B_2018"
  ],
  "depth": 10,
  "settings": {
   "ndvi": 0.12
  },
  "check": "16 Nov 2026, 20 single-family lots in Pearland/Manvel (Brazoria file), pictures looked at: Colorado's green line (NDVI 0.33) read 0 lawn on 17 of 20 lots. In the Texas NAIP 2022 photo plainly green St. Augustine/Bermuda reads NDVI 0.10-0.25 and tree crowns under ~0.33 (the 2024 photo is from Oct-Nov 2024 and shows dormant Bermuda: not used first). Roofs and paving read below 0, bare ground 0.05-0.10. At 0.12 the lawns read 300-6,600 sq ft on 3,300-15,000 sq ft lots and match the pictures (front and back lawns counted, houses, drives and shrub beds left out). Known gap: the 2018 laser predates houses built since (fast-growing suburbs), so on those lots its tree crowns and roof can be stale; the photo still decides what is green.",
  "note": "Yard v2 lawn measuring (metros/yard-metro.mjs, V2 settings). Photos: NAIP Texas 2022 (13-21 Sep 2022, late summer by the classifier's rule) first, 2024 (10 Oct - 19 Nov 2024) second; the classifier uses the late-summer one. Laser: USGS 3DEP 2018 coastal surveys on the public Entwine bucket, depth 10 (1.4-1.6 m tiles, like Colorado's 1.2-1.8 m). No DRCOG (Denver only)."
 },
